Write a proposal page for: $ARGUMENTS

The readers are busy people on another team. They read the page once, and then they decide, build
or answer. Less is more: the page holds only what they need for that, in plain words. Everything
else (the evidence, the detail, how you found it) stays in the proposal's working files.

Change nothing in the project: the only files you write are under `.claude/notes/proposals/`. The
page is in English unless I ask for another language. You publish it as a private Artifact, and you
never send it anywhere.

{{ guidance }}

**How those rules fit a proposal.** The evidence lives in `facts.md`, one row per fact the page
relies on: the fact, its evidence (a `file:line`, a document, or the command and what it printed),
and its tag: Verified (code, config or a command shows it), Documented (a document says it and
nothing checked it), or Inferred, with your honest estimate in percent. The page carries no
citations. A fact the page relies on that isn't Verified shows `(to confirm)`. If the readers can
answer it, it becomes a question for the readers; if only we can, it goes under Risks and open
points with who confirms it. What we propose (a format, a limit, a status code) is a choice, not a
fact: say it plainly. A path, field or endpoint you made up to show the idea is marked `example`.

## Working files

Work in small steps, one file each, so an interrupted session picks up where it stopped. Each
proposal has its own folder, `.claude/notes/proposals/<slug>/`; the slug is ASCII `a-z`, `0-9` and
hyphens, in English, at most 60 characters, naming the change (`vendor-connect`).

| File | Holds |
|---|---|
| `facts.md` | The readers and what we need from them, the facts, what is to confirm, the diagrams chosen |
| `outline.md` | The page's text only, in Markdown: headings, lines, table cells, questions, where each diagram goes |
| `spec.md` | Only when they build: the exact names they must match (paths, fields, headers, codes), at most 12 lines |
| `diagrams/<kind>-<name>.svg` | One diagram per file, such as `diagrams/sequence-api-does-the-work.svg` |
| `<slug>.html` | The page, put together from the files above: what gets published |

`<kind>` is one of `context`, `container`, `deployment`, `network`, `er`, `sequence`, `flowchart`;
`<name>` is the option's slug, or what the diagram shows when it holds for every option. Read each
file by its exact path: search tools skip a git-ignored folder. `facts.md` starts as:

````markdown
# <slug>
Readers: <team> · Need: <decision, build, answers: one or more> · By: <date, or none yet>
Commit: <git rev-parse --short HEAD, or none outside git> · Updated: <date>
Artifact: <the link, once published>

## Facts
| Fact | Evidence | Tag |
|---|---|---|

## To confirm
- <what the page marks (to confirm), and who can answer it: the readers, or who on our side>

## Diagrams
- <kind>-<name>: <the question it answers for the readers>
````

## 0. Start
1. Run `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-dir --git-common-dir`. If the two differ, you are
   in a linked worktree: the main checkout's proposals aren't visible here, and the folder you write
   is copied there. Say so in one line. Outside a git repository, carry on: write `Commit: none`
   and skip the git commands.
2. Read `.claude/notes/proposals/.gitignore`; if it doesn't exist, write it with the single line
   `*`, which keeps the folder out of git.
3. Then:
   - **Nothing asked:** run `ls -t .claude/notes/proposals/` and, for each folder, show its title,
     its last step and its `Artifact:` link. Ask which to continue, or what to propose.
   - **A slug or a title that matches:** read that folder's files first, because I may have edited
     them and the files win over your memory. Then continue from the first step whose file is
     missing, or make the change I asked for and say in one line what changed.
   - **Anything else:** a new proposal in a new folder.

## 1. Ask only what's missing
Never ask what the request, the code or the docs answer: look first, and write what you inferred in
`facts.md`. Then ask one question at a time, at most 3 questions in all, each with 2–4 options, the
recommended one first with a one-line reason:
- who reads it, and what we need from them: a decision, something built, answers, or more than one;
- the options, if I have some in mind; otherwise propose 2, at most 3;
- a constraint the code can't show: a date (or none yet), a cost, a no-go.

## 2. Facts → `facts.md`
Read-only: the README, docs, decision records and runbooks; the code of the parts the proposal
touches; their infrastructure definitions (Terraform, CloudFormation, CDK, Kubernetes, compose),
config, schemas and migrations; `git log` for what changed lately. Read in parallel with
{{ explorer }} subagents when there is much to read; a few short documents, read yourself. Write
each fact into `facts.md` as you find it. One that matters but can't be checked goes under
`To confirm`.

## 3. Choose the diagrams
Pick only the diagrams the readers need: each one answers a question the text would otherwise take
a paragraph for. Usually 2 or 3; never one of each kind for its own sake.

| Diagram | Answers | Draw it when the proposal |
|---|---|---|
| System context | Who and what talks to the system? | adds or changes an outside party: users, a vendor, another team's system |
| Container / component | Which parts are inside, and which one changes? | adds or changes a service, store, queue or module |
| Deployment | Where does each part run? | changes accounts, regions, clusters, hosts or environments |
| Network | How does traffic get in and out, and through which gate? | changes subnets, gateways, firewalls, endpoints, or who can reach what |
| Entity-relationship (ER) | Which records exist, and how do they relate? | adds or changes stored data |
| Sequence | Who calls whom, in what order, and what happens to the data? | changes a request, a hand-off, or a data flow and its transformations |
| Flowchart / activity | Which path does a case take, and where does it branch? | has decisions, retries or failure paths |

A diagram that holds for every option goes once, under Today → Goal. Draw a diagram per option only
where the options differ, with the same kind for each, so they compare side by side. Draw what the
proposal adds or changes, against today, in the `new` style; on a flow that is new from end to end,
nothing is `new`. Write the list into `facts.md`, one line each on why it's there.

## 4. Text → `outline.md`
Write the page's text in this order. Drop a section with nothing to say, except In short, Options
and Questions. Mark where each diagram goes with `![<the question it answers>](diagrams/<file>.svg)`.
1. **Header:** a kicker (system · area), a title of at most 8 words naming the change, a lead of at
   most 20 words (what changes, for whom), and `Proposed · not built · As of <date>`.
2. **In short:** three lines. We propose … / We recommend "<the option's name>", because … (or We
   chose …, when the choice is ours, not the readers') / We need from <team>: …, by <date, or "a
   date you set">.
3. **Today → Goal:** one line each, and the diagrams that hold for every option.
4. **Options:** 2 or 3. Each has a name that says what it is ("The API does the work"), never a
   letter or a number: the page and the comparison call it by that name, and its slug names its
   diagram files. Each has one line, its diagrams, and at most 3 bullets the table doesn't already
   say. Mark the recommended one. When the choice is ours, show the chosen option and say in one
   line what we ruled out.
5. **Compare:** a table with only the rows where the options differ, at most 12 words a cell. What
   is the same in all goes in one line under it, in words the page has already used.
6. **What we ask <team> to do:** at most 7 numbered lines for the recommended option, each something
   they can check is done; `spec.md` follows as a code block.
7. **Questions for <team>:** at most 5, only what they can answer, each answered by yes, no or one
   value; a question with two parts is two questions.
8. **Risks and open points:** at most 5, each "risk → what we do about it"; our own open points say
   who confirms them.
9. **Sources:** only what the readers can open, each with what it says in a few words; none, no
   section.

**Less is more:**
- Every line helps the readers decide, build or answer. Cut anything else.
- One fact per sentence, at most 20 words. Plain words, active voice, the system as the subject:
  "The API checks the key".
- A term the readers may not know: use plain words instead, or explain it in at most 6 words the
  first time.
- One name for one thing, the same on every line and in every diagram.
- No bare step numbers or codes: "the key-write call (step 9)", not "step 9"; a decision record
  with what it decided, not its number alone.
- Don't retell a diagram in words, tell how we got here, or hedge.
- Over a limit, cut words; never shrink the type.

When `outline.md` is written, run `wc -w` on it: at most 550, which leaves about 500 words of text,
because `wc` counts the Markdown signs (`|`, `-`, `##`) too. Over that, cut and count again.

## 5. Diagrams → `diagrams/`
Read `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/diagrams.md` now. Draw one diagram at a time, in its own file, with the
recipe for its kind, and check it against the recipe's checklist before you start the next. Its
labels use the outline's names, word for word; a part that only a diagram shows needs no line of
text.

## 6. Cold read
Start a fresh, read-only subagent ({{ explorer }}; it gets no conversation context) with the paths of
`outline.md`, `spec.md` if there is one, and the SVG files, and this: *"Read only these files. You are
on <readers>, and you see this proposal for the first time. Answer in one line each: what changes;
which option is recommended, and why; what is asked of whom, by when. Then list every sentence or
label you had to read twice or could not follow, and every term that isn't explained. Write `none`
when nothing is unclear."* A wrong answer means the page is unclear there: fix that first. Then fix
the items it lists, in the outline and the diagrams; one that needs more room than the limits allow
goes under `To confirm` instead. Run it again: at most 2 rounds in all, and after the second, fix
only what takes a few words. If no subagent can be started, read those files cold yourself and say
so in the hand-off. Last, compare with `facts.md`: if a fact the readers need to act is gone, put it
back in as few words as it takes.

## 7. Page → `<slug>.html`
If I called the material confidential or sensitive, ask before you go on: the next calls reach the
Artifact service. If an `Artifact` tool is in this session (load it with ToolSearch if it is listed as
deferred), call it with `action: "quickstart"` and `intent: "other"` first: its page rules win where
they differ from the template. Then copy `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/page.html` and fill it from the outline,
`spec.md` and the SVG files, pasted in as they are. Add no content of your own: a change goes into
the outline or the diagram first. The `<title>` is the page's name, 2 to 4 words naming the change
("One-Button Vendor Connect"), or the name I already use for it; never "Proposal: …". Each figure's
caption is the question its diagram answers. If you can open the page in a browser, look at it in
light and dark and at phone width, and fix whatever overlaps or is cut off; if you can't, say so in
the hand-off.

## 8. Publish a private Artifact
Read the page once more for what must not leave this machine: no keys, tokens, passwords, account
IDs or IP addresses, and no logo or brand styling of a real company.
- **First time:** publish `.claude/notes/proposals/<slug>/<slug>.html` with no `url`, with
  `icon: "diagram"` and a one-sentence `description`: what we propose and which option we recommend.
  Write the link into `facts.md` as `Artifact: <url>` straight away.
- **Again** (`facts.md` has an `Artifact:` line): read that artifact first with `action: "read"` and
  bring any change made there into your files, then publish with that same `url`, so the readers'
  link stays the same.
- The artifact is private, and I share it. Never share, pin or delete it, and never send it anywhere.
- **No `Artifact` tool** (a session without it): say so, give the path of `<slug>.html`, and tell me
  that `/proposal <slug>` in a session with the tool publishes it.

## 9. Hand off
Give the artifact's link, the `(to confirm)` items and what you couldn't check, and how to make a
PDF: open `<slug>.html` in a browser, then Print → Save as PDF (an Artifact can't open the print
dialog). Then stop.
